From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SheepyLord <37598021+SheepyLord@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 11:46:08 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Lock RtxOption edits made beside the render thread's resolve

An R13 PoC session died 15 s into idle: an access violation reading 0x50
in RtxOptionImpl::resolveValue under RtxOptionManager::applyPendingValues
on the render thread. 0x50 is m_type, read through a null this: the dirty
option map handed the loop a nullptr. An entry holds nullptr only while
another thread is inside markDirty's operator[] (the node is linked before
the option is stored in it), or once concurrent changes corrupted the map.

remixapi_SetConfigVariable is that thread. It calls readOption, which
inserted into the option's layer values, wrote the value and marked the
option dirty holding only the API's own mutex, on the game's thread; the
companion's sky sends its changed values every 20 ms. A unit test that
does the same while another thread resolves crashes the old code within
two seconds at the same instruction, with the same registers.

Whatever reads or writes option layer values or the dirty map now holds
the update mutex (order: the layer mutex, the update mutex, the registry
mutexes):

- readOption (the Remix API) and clearFromStrongerLayers (the menu's
  Unblock, deprecated-option migrations in onChange callbacks) take it;
- acquireLayer and releaseLayer take it after the layer mutex, as
  applyPendingValues does: the graph's option layer actions changed the
  registry and every option's values beside the readers;
- the menu, which draws on the game's thread: Discard Changes (reload),
  save, Delete All, the migrations, the unsaved-change and misplaced
  counts, forEachChange; its Option Layers list holds the layer mutex;
- graphicsPresetOnChange's move of the Quality layer to the User layer;
- logEffectiveValues.

applyPendingValues also lost edits made on other threads. It unlocked
between passes even with no callback to run, so a burst of edits kept it
going to maxResolves, and the final clear, taken after a moment without
the lock, dropped the last marks unresolved: such an edit took effect only
when the option changed again. It now keeps the lock unless a callback
runs, and holds it to the final clear.

test_rtx_option_threads runs the API's writes, layer churn and the menu's
actions against a resolving thread and checks that every write resolves.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
